On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 09:51:59 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> A couple things... I am getting rid of the lists folder. As discussed in
> another thread, the folders are now:
>
> - INBOX
> - Archive
> - Later
> - Next
> - quarantine
>
> Additionally, I want lab-mail to sync all folders with fastmail. It's
> only the mailing-list clients that limit to those folders.

Done in "[PATCH v9 00/10] jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the list's
mail hub" [1]. Patches 1 to 8 are unchanged from v8.

- Patch 9, "[PATCH v9 09/10] jails/instances/lab-mail: Deliver posts
  to INBOX and sync it", has mbsync on lab-mail sync every folder
  (Patterns *) with Create Near. A folder you make on Fastmail shows
  up on lab-mail at the next sync, with no change to lab-mail. To
  allow that, mbsync's ACL entry gets the create right back. lists is
  gone from the config, the README, and the spec, and Dovecot no
  longer creates it.
- Patch 10, "[PATCH v9 10/10] jails/instances/lab-mail: Spec the
  Next, Later, and quarantine folders", is the client side. Each
  client's mbsync names INBOX, Next, Later, Archive, and quarantine,
  and nothing else. The folder is a message's only state, and notmuch
  derives the next, later, archived, and quarantined tags from it.

Patch 10 replaces "[PATCH] jails/instances/lab-mail: Sync the Next
and Later folders" [2]. Once lab-mail syncs every folder it has
nothing to name, so that patch is superseded and can be dropped.

You make Next, Later, and quarantine on Fastmail. If mail is still in
lists there, mbsync will keep carrying it like any other folder until
you delete the folder.

> Some process-mail changes as well:
>
> - only process messages tagged with inbox. This is how I can archive
>   messages and not have them be processed.

The loop already picks only tag:inbox, in oldest_inbox_message() in
src/lab-process-mail/lab-process-mail. The catch is that mail-tag.sh
recomputes inbox on every notmuch new, so removing the tag by hand does
not stick. The next notmuch new puts it back. Archiving only keeps a
message out if the tag comes from the folder. "[PATCH v2 1/2] notmuch:
Tag messages under archive/ as archived" [3], from "[PATCH v2 0/2]
Archive messages that need no answer" [4], does that for the jj maildir:
a file under archive/ is tagged archived and never inbox. That series is
still open. Applying it is what makes "archive and it is not processed"
work today. On lab-mail the same rule becomes the Archive folder, as
patch 10 above specs.

> - do not act on any messages other than from approved senders. If a
>   message from a non-approved sender makes it through, move it to
>   quarantine folder.

Posted as "[PATCH 0/2] Quarantine mail from unapproved senders" [5].

- Patch 1 tags every message under the list's quarantine/ maildir
  quarantined and keeps it out of the inbox. Moving the file back
  releases it.
- Patch 2 checks the sender in the loop, before the agent runs, so
  the check does not depend on the model. The From must name exactly
  one address, and that address must match a line of the senders file
  in full, ignoring case. Any other message is moved into
  quarantine/, the move is committed and pushed on my bookmark like a
  reply, you get a "quarantined:" ntfy notice, and the loop goes on.
  Without a senders file the loop refuses to start.

The packaged senders file lists pat@patmaddox.com and pat@p7x.dev.
You have also posted once from patmaddox@FreeBSD.org. Should it be
on the list? Until it is, mail from that address is quarantined.

Two limits. The check reads the From header, so it stops stray mail
but not a forged From. Stopping that needs lab-mail or Fastmail to
verify the sender first. And quarantine moves files in the jj
maildir, since that is what lab-process-mail reads today. Once it
reads the list from lab-mail, the move becomes an IMAP MOVE, and
claude@p7x.dev will need the right to move into quarantine.

